      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htChartMsg\"></span></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nWe recommend studying the previous texts in this section before continuing, especially the one in which we learned that multiplication is a **repeated sum**, and the ones in which we learned to multiply with the abacuses and the Yupana. They will help you understand the foundations we build upon here.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1da73b2-5d6a-4343-98e9-2d9be753b123",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Multiplying with Soroban, Suanpan (算盤) and Schyoty (счёты)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c4c2c00d-7878-42da-9cc4-060cd7f8d225",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Yupana (multiplication)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWe already know how to multiply numbers in many ways: with our hands, with the Yupana, and with the abacuses. We also learned that multiplication is a **repeated sum**—adding the same number over and over. But did you know that we can also *draw* a multiplication table?\n\nIn this text, we will learn a simple recipe with three steps:\n1. First, we create a **formula** that describes the multiplication table.\n2. Then, we **plot points** for that formula.\n3. Finally, we **connect the points**.\n\nWhen we connect the points, beautiful shapes appear—straight lines and even triangles!",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_formula",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### First Step: Creating a Formula\n\nA formula is a short way of writing a rule with numbers. Instead of writing many sums, we use a letter to represent the numbers that can change.\n\nIn the text *Adding the same numbers*, we learned that multiplication is a **repeated sum** (<htcite0>): when we add the same number many times, we can write it in a shorter way. For example, instead of writing 10 + 10 + 10, we can write 3 × 10.\n\nWe can write this idea with a single formula that works for any multiplication (<htcite1>)",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "79d126c9-39ac-4679-904b-4ab69558e44e",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>r</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: General formula for multiplication tables.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this formula:\n- **r**: is the result of the multiplication (the product).\n- **N**: is the number of times we repeat the same number (the factor).\n- **a**: is the number we repeat—the table itself. It is also called the **angular coefficient** (or **slope**).\n\nWhen **a** is small, the results stay close to the horizontal line. When **a** grows, the results climb higher and higher.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now let's see the formula in action with the simplest values of **a**:\n- When **a = 0**, we repeat the number zero: 0 + 0 + 0 + ... + 0 = 0 × **N**. Every result is zero.\n- When **a = 1**, we repeat the number one: 1 + 1 + 1 + ... + 1 = 1 × **N**. Every result is the number **N** itself.\n\nMove the pointer over each result to see how it is formed",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>Applying the formula with **a = 0**, we get the multiplication table of zero (<b>Table 1</b>):</p><p><div id=\"mParent0\"  style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>Applying the formula with **a = 1**, we get the multiplication table of one (<b>Table 2</b>):</p><p><div id=\"mParent1\"  style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_points",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Second Step: Plotting the Points\n\nA formula gives us numbers, but numbers alone do not show us the shape of a multiplication table. To see its shape, we need to draw.\n\nWe use two lines, called **axes**:\n- The **horizontal axis** shows the values of **N** (the numbers we count).\n- The **vertical axis** shows the results **r** (the products).\n\nFor each value of **N**, we place a single point: we find the value of **N** on the horizontal axis, then the result on the vertical axis, and we mark the meeting place with a dot.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Making a Prediction</b><br /><br />When **a = 0**, every result is zero.<br /><br />Where do you expect the points to be on the chart?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Let's plot the points for **a = 0**, following our recipe:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart1\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 1</b>: Points from the multiplication table of zero. Chart created using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As we predicted, every point sits on the horizontal axis, because every result is zero. Now let's plot the points for **a = 1**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart2\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 2</b>: Points from the multiplication table of one. Chart created using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The points for **a = 1** step upward, one unit at a time, forming a **diagonal** path across the squared paper.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_connect",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Third Step: Connecting the Points\n\nFinally, we connect neighboring points with a straight stroke. This is where the shapes appear!\n\nFor **a = 0**, all the points are at the same height, so connecting them gives us a flat, horizontal line:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart3\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 3</b>: Connected points of the multiplication table of zero, forming a flat line. Chart created using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "For **a = 1**, the connection follows the diagonal path of the points and gives us a rising straight line. This line divides the squared paper into two equal parts:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart4\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 4</b>: Connected points of the multiplication table of one, forming a rising line. Chart created using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We can also show the case **a = 1** with boxes. Each column has as many boxes as its product:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart5\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 5</b>: Boxes of the same color showing the multiplication table of one. Each column of boxes represents a result: 1 × 1 is one box, 2 × 1 is two boxes, and so on. Chart created using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Flat or Rising?</b><br /><br />When we connect the points for **a = 0**, we obtain a flat line. When we connect the points for **a = 1**, we obtain a rising line.<br /><br />Why are these two lines so different?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_triangle",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Triangle\n\nBefore proceeding, to further clarify the concepts introduced earlier and what we will explore next, we have filled the lower triangle formed by the connected points of the multiplication table of one",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart6\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 6</b>: Points of the multiplication table connected, forming a straight line <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: Defining the Triangle</b><br /><br />Looking at Chart 6, where the triangular area is highlighted, how would you describe this shape?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Compare your definition with a simplified version of the one provided by Eric W. Weisstein",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f33dbf7c-924c-453f-bee5-a19388e75109",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W., Triangle",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<ul class=\"citation\"><li><i>\"A triangle is a closed shape with three sides and three angles (formed by the intersection of its sides)...\"</i></li> </ul>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Beyond this general definition, Weisstein notes that the right triangle shown in the chart has specific components with distinct names. In Chart 7, we identify these elements and label them",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f33dbf7c-924c-453f-bee5-a19388e75109",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W., Triangle",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "56edda64-d96e-4106-b1f8-676a235b818c",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W., Right Triangle",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg height=\"245\" width=\"450\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M425 5 L75 200 L425 200 Z\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:green;stroke-width:3\" /><rect x=\"404\" y=\"179\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" stroke=\"green\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><text x=\"125\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"1.5em\">α</text><text x=\"401\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"1.5em\">β</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"2.5em\">c</text><text x=\"428\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"2.5em\">b</text><text x=\"265\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"2.5em\">a</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 7</b>: Right triangle with its components labeled.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **a**, **b** and **c**: The sides of the triangle. Sides **a** and **b** are called **legs**, while **c** is the **hypotenuse**.\n- **α**, **β**, and <i class=\"fa-regular fa-square\"></i>: The angles formed by the intersection of the sides. The square symbol ( <i class=\"fa-regular fa-square\"></i> ) marking the angle between the horizontal and vertical sides is called the **right angle**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we learned a recipe with three steps that works for any multiplication table:\n1. Create a **formula**.\n2. **Plot the points**.\n3. **Connect the points**.\n\nWith this recipe, the table of zero became a flat line, and the table of one became a rising line. When we filled the space between the line of the table of one and the axes, we discovered a very important shape: the **triangle**.\n\nIn the next texts, we will use this recipe with other tables and discover new shapes.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Is a triangle a closed shape with three sides and three angles?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "This is the basic definition of a triangle that we are using in this chapter, but there are additional aspects that will further develop this concept in subsequent texts."
      },
      {
         "question": "In the multiplication table of zero, do all the points lie on the horizontal axis?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "Multiplying any number by zero always gives zero. Therefore, every point we plot is at height zero, aligned with the horizontal axis."
      },
      {
         "question": "In the multiplication table of one, do the connected points form a flat, horizontal line?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "The points of the multiplication table of one grow one unit at a time, so when we connect them they form a rising diagonal line. Only the table of zero produces a flat line."
      }
   ],
   "game_v2": null,
   "date_time": null
}
